 succeeded in 452ms:
     1	# ADR-0114: Scoring Unification — Shared Primitives, Per-Composer Surfaces
     2	
     3	**Status:** Proposed
     4	**Date:** 2026-04-19
     5	**Target:** v1.4.0 substrate (shared factor library, trust extractor) / v1.6.0 (health composer migration)
     6	**Extends:** [ADR-0097](0097-account-health-scoring-architecture.md)
     7	**Related:** [ADR-0080](0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md), [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md), [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md), [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md), [ADR-0113](0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md)
     8	**Consumed by:** [DOS-5](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-5) Trust Compiler, [DOS-10](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-10) Freshness decay, [DOS-238](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-238) Factor library implementation
     9	
    10	## Context
    11	
    12	DailyOS has two composers that produce scores from entity signals: the **Trust Compiler** ([DOS-5](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-5)) which scores per-claim trustworthiness for the provenance envelope, and the **Account Health Composer** ([ADR-0097](0097-account-health-scoring-architecture.md)) which scores per-dimension health for briefings. As drafted, both reimplement the same primitive calculations — source reliability from Bayesian posteriors, freshness decay, corroboration boosts, contradiction penalties, user-feedback modulation. The math is identical; only the composition differs (weighted geometric mean for trust, lifecycle-weighted arithmetic for health).
    13	
    14	Duplicating factor math is a correctness hazard. A fix to the freshness decay function in the trust path that does not also land in the health path produces silently divergent behavior — a claim that is "stale" for trust but "fresh" for health. Worse, the six health dimensions ([ADR-0097](0097-account-health-scoring-architecture.md)) already include freshness-like decay inside their composition, creating three implementations at risk of drifting.
    15	
    16	An adversarial review during v1.4.0 planning surfaced a second concern: "pure factors" cannot take an `EntityId` parameter and still be pure. A function that takes an ID implicitly reads the database, carries a hidden clock, or depends on global state — all of which break determinism under [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)'s `Evaluate` mode and defeat [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md)'s fixture-replay guarantees.
    17	
    18	This ADR specifies the shared factor library, the per-composer extractor pattern, what stays inside each composer, and the confidence evidence structure that propagates to the UI. The goal is one source of truth per primitive, zero hidden state, and deterministic behavior under fixed inputs.
    19	
    20	## Decision
    21	
    22	### 1. Shared primitive factors
    23	
    24	A single module, `src-tauri/src/scoring/factors.rs`, contains pure functions for cross-composer primitives. Every function in this module:
    25	
    26	- Takes explicit typed inputs; no `EntityId`, no `&AbilityContext`, no `&Db`, no globals.
    27	- Takes any clock-dependent quantity (e.g., "age of this claim") pre-computed by the caller, not a clock reference.
    28	- Is deterministic: the same inputs always produce the same output.
    29	- Is side-effect free.
    30	- Is tested with table-driven property tests that cover boundary cases.
    31	
    32	The canonical set is small and closed — new factors require an ADR amendment.
    33	
    34	```rust
    35	// src-tauri/src/scoring/factors.rs
    36	
    37	pub fn source_reliability(beta_alpha: f64, beta_beta: f64) -> f64;
    38	
    39	pub fn freshness_weight(
    40	    source: &DataSource,
    41	    age_days: f64,
    42	    ctx: &FreshnessContext,
    43	) -> f64;
    44	
    45	pub fn corroboration_weight(independent_source_count: u32) -> f64;
    46	
    47	pub fn contradiction_penalty(flags: &ContradictionSet) -> f64;
    48	
    49	pub fn user_feedback_weight(feedback: Option<&UserFeedback>) -> f64;
    50	
    51	pub fn meeting_relevance_weight(
    52	    signal_type_affinity: f64,
    53	    meeting_context_match: f64,
    54	    attendee_overlap: f64,
    55	) -> f64;
    56	```
    57	
    58	Supporting types, also pure:
    59	
    60	```rust
    61	pub struct FreshnessContext {
    62	    pub upcoming_renewal_within_days: Option<u32>,
    63	    pub claim_type: ClaimType,
    64	}
    65	
    66	pub struct ContradictionSet {
    67	    pub coherence_check_failed: bool,
    68	    pub peer_contradictions: u32,
    69	}
    70	
    71	pub struct UserFeedback {
    72	    pub action: FeedbackAction,        // StillTrue | Outdated | WrongSource
    73	    pub at: DateTime<Utc>,             // Caller pre-computes age if needed
    74	}
    75	```
    76	
    77	Absent from this list: anything that requires entity graph traversal, any aggregation across multiple claims, any composition rule. Those belong in composers.
    78	
    79	### 2. Per-surface extractors
    80	
    81	Each composer has its own extractor that reads the database, injects the clock, and produces the typed factor inputs.
    82	
    83	**Trust extractor:** `src-tauri/src/scoring/extract/trust.rs`
    84	
    85	```rust
    86	pub async fn extract_trust_inputs(
    87	    claim: &Claim,
    88	    ctx: &ServiceContext<'_>,
    89	) -> Result<TrustScoringInputs, Error>;
    90	
    91	pub struct TrustScoringInputs {
    92	    pub source_alpha: f64,
    93	    pub source_beta: f64,
    94	    pub source: DataSource,
    95	    pub age_days: f64,                            // Computed from ctx.clock.now() - claim.created_at
    96	    pub freshness_ctx: FreshnessContext,
    97	    pub independent_source_count: u32,
    98	    pub contradictions: ContradictionSet,
    99	    pub feedback: Option<UserFeedback>,
   100	    pub meeting_relevance: MeetingRelevanceInputs,
   101	}
   102	```
   103	
   104	**Health extractor:** remains co-located with the health composer at `src-tauri/src/intelligence/health_scoring.rs`. Per-dimension extractors produce dimension-specific input structs that call the shared primitives where applicable.
   105	
   106	Extractors are where database access and clock injection happen. Factors never touch either. Extractors are tested with fixture-loaded databases; factors are tested with table-driven pure inputs.
   107	
   108	### 3. Relationship dimensions stay in `health::dimensions`
   109	
   110	The six health dimensions from [ADR-0097](0097-account-health-scoring-architecture.md) — `meeting_cadence`, `email_engagement`, `stakeholder_coverage`, `champion_health`, `financial_proximity`, `signal_momentum` — are **not** promoted into `scoring::factors`. They are health-composer-internal compositions that may call shared primitives internally but express dimension-specific semantics (thresholds, lifecycle weights, per-dimension decay curves) that are not meaningful outside health.
   111	
   112	Rationale: `meeting_cadence` is not a universal primitive — "are meetings happening often enough?" depends on stage, segment, and contract value. Trust scoring has no equivalent. Promoting it would add surface area without sharing.
   113	
   114	Health dimensions MAY invoke shared primitives like `freshness_weight` and `contradiction_penalty` from within their own composition. They may not maintain their own implementations of these — if a dimension needs a tweaked variant, the variant is either (a) a config parameter to the shared factor, or (b) a local helper in the dimension module that clearly documents why the shared factor didn't fit.
   115	
   116	### 4. Per-composer combinators
   117	
   118	Composers combine factors their own way. This is the point of the split.
   119	
   120	**Trust Compiler** ([DOS-5](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-5)): weighted geometric mean over six factors.
   121	
   122	```rust
   123	trust_score = exp(Σ w_i × log(factor_i))
   124	```
   125	
   126	Weights, clamps, and bands live in `config/trust_compiler.toml`. The Trust Compiler reads primitives from `scoring::factors` and composes; it does not reimplement any primitive.
   127	
   128	**Account Health Composer** ([ADR-0097](0097-account-health-scoring-architecture.md)): lifecycle-weighted arithmetic mean over the six dimensions.
   129	
   130	```rust
   131	dimension_score = weighted_composition_of_dimension_specific_signals
   132	account_health = Σ lifecycle_weight(i) × dimension_score(i)
   133	```
   134	
   135	Lifecycle weights, dimension thresholds, and confidence adjustments are health-composer internal.
   136	
   137	Future composers (e.g., a v1.6.0+ "meeting priority" score, a v2.x "portfolio alert" score) will likewise have their own combinators and reuse `scoring::factors`.
   138	
   139	### 5. Confidence evidence, not shared bands
   140	

 succeeded in 452ms:
   140	
   141	Each composer produces its own band taxonomy — trust uses `likely_current | use_with_caution | needs_verification`, health uses a dimension-specific color coding, future composers will define their own. Bands are not shared; forcing a shared band taxonomy would couple unrelated surfaces to a single scale.
   142	
   143	What **is** shared: a `ConfidenceEvidence` struct that every composer emits alongside its score, consumed uniformly by UI rendering.
   144	
   145	```rust
   146	pub struct ConfidenceEvidence {
   147	    pub score: f64,                      // The composer's score, normalized [0, 1]
   148	    pub band_label: String,              // Composer-defined band name
   149	    pub factor_breakdown: Vec<FactorEvidence>,
   150	    pub caveats: Vec<ConfidenceCaveat>,
   151	}
   152	
   153	pub struct FactorEvidence {
   154	    pub name: &'static str,              // "freshness", "corroboration", ...
   155	    pub value: f64,
   156	    pub contribution: f64,               // Weight × log(value) for geometric; weight × value for arithmetic
   157	}
   158	
   159	pub enum ConfidenceCaveat {
   160	    FewSources,
   161	    StaleSource { source: DataSource, age_days: f64 },
   162	    UnresolvedContradiction,
   163	    InsufficientSignalDensity,
   164	}
   165	```
   166	
   167	UI surfaces render `ConfidenceEvidence` uniformly: a score, a band, an expandable breakdown, and a list of caveats. Bands differ per composer; the rendering shell is common. This is what [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md) consumes.
   168	
   169	### 6. Determinism and mode awareness
   170	
   171	Factors are pure by construction. Extractors use the injected clock from [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) for any age computation. Under `ExecutionMode::Evaluate`:
   172	
   173	- Extractors hit the fixture-loaded SQLite per [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md).
   174	- Clock is the fixture's `clock.txt`.
   175	- Factor outputs are fully deterministic given fixture inputs.
   176	- Composer outputs are fully deterministic given factor outputs.
   177	
   178	This means both trust scores and health scores are replayable in the evaluation harness with no additional work beyond fixture creation. Regression tests for scoring changes become cheap.
   179	
   180	### 7. Evaluation
   181	
   182	`src-tauri/tests/scoring/` holds the factor-level property tests:
   183	
   184	- Each factor has a table-driven test with ≥10 boundary and typical cases.
   185	- Factor output ranges, monotonicity, and clamp behavior are property-tested against 10K random inputs.
   186	- Extractors have fixture-based tests that verify correct input assembly from representative DB states.
   187	- Composers have evaluation-harness fixtures ([ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md)) verifying end-to-end score outputs against hand-computed expected values.
   188	
   189	### 8. Config
   190	
   191	Scoring-related configuration consolidates under one namespace:
   192	
   193	```
   194	config/scoring.toml
   195	├── [trust]
   196	│   ├── weights = { ... }
   197	│   ├── clamps = { ... }
   198	│   └── bands = { ... }
   199	├── [health]
   200	│   ├── lifecycle_weights = { ... }
   201	│   └── dimension_thresholds = { ... }
   202	└── [factors]
   203	    ├── freshness_half_lives = { ... }   # consumed by freshness_weight
   204	    └── corroboration_scale = 0.5          # consumed by corroboration_weight
   205	```
   206	
   207	Boot-time validation: all weights sum where required, no thresholds outside [0, 1], and composers fail-fast on malformed config.
   208	
   209	## Consequences
   210	
   211	### Positive
   212	
   213	- **One source of truth per primitive.** Fixing `freshness_weight` lands everywhere automatically. Drift between trust and health becomes impossible by construction.
   214	- **Determinism preserved.** Pure factors are replayable in the evaluation harness; no hidden state, no implicit DB reads.
   215	- **Composers stay expressive.** Each composer keeps its own combinator — geometric mean, lifecycle weighting, whatever future surfaces need — without being forced into a shared scale.
   216	- **UI rendering uniform.** One `ConfidenceEvidence` shape means the product's explanation affordance works identically for trust and health without cross-coupling their semantics.
   217	- **Adversarial review finding addressed.** No factor takes `EntityId`; purity is structural, not conventional.
   218	- **Factor library is small and closed.** Six primitives, each well-bounded. Growth requires ADR amendment — forces us to be deliberate about what becomes universal.
   219	
   220	### Negative / risks
   221	
   222	- **Extractors are real work per composer.** Each new composer pays a boilerplate cost to assemble inputs. Accepted — the cost is one-time per surface and keeps factor-level code clean.
   223	- **Refactoring health to use shared primitives is deferred.** The v1.4.0 cycle ships the library + trust extractor only. Health composer migration is v1.6.0 Hardening. During this window, health and trust compute freshness separately; drift is possible and must be caught by cross-composer property tests.
   224	- **"Why isn't `meeting_cadence` shared?" is a recurring question.** The answer is consistent: health dimensions are compositions, not primitives. Reviewers and future contributors will want a shared "relationship scorer." Resist it — it is semantic coupling disguised as reuse.
   225	- **Config namespace split requires migration.** Existing `config/trust_compiler.toml` references merge into `config/scoring.toml` with backward-compatible alias. Ships behind a feature flag.
   226	
   227	### Neutral
   228	
   229	- No user-visible change. This is internal architecture.
   230	- `DataSource` taxonomy unchanged. [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md) continues to own source classification; this ADR consumes it.
   231	- `scoring::factors` is a new module but does not grow over time. A healthy sign.
   232	
   233	---
