.docs/plans/_archive/v0.13.5-shipped.md:3:**Thesis:** People are the most under-represented entities in DailyOS. A user managing a buying committee knows five individuals by name but sees them as independent contacts with no structural relationship to each other. They don't see that the champion is locked out of decisions because of a blocker, or that the executive sponsor will only approve what the technical evaluator signs off on. This version adds typed relationship networks for people — a qualitatively different intelligence model because people don't form trees, they form relationship graphs. It builds a `person_relationships` table with directional, context-scoped edges (champion, blocker, peer, ally, etc.), extends person enrichment to include relationship context, adds person→person signal propagation so relationship changes ripple through the network, and surfaces the network as a new chapter on person detail pages with AI-synthesized cluster summaries, risks, and opportunities. (ADR-0088)
.docs/plans/_archive/v0.13.5-shipped.md:78:- **Edge-type-sensitive multipliers** (ADR-0088): The rule function needs a `match` on `relationship_type` to apply modifiers (1.0x for champion/sponsor/decision_maker, 1.2x for blocker/detractor, 0.7x for peer/ally, 0.0x for collaborator below threshold, context-gated for dependency). More nuanced than the existing hierarchy rules (flat 0.6/0.5).
.docs/plans/_archive/v0.13.5-shipped.md:101:2. All 10 relationship types are valid enum values: `champion`, `executive_sponsor`, `decision_maker`, `technical_evaluator`, `blocker`, `peer`, `ally`, `detractor`, `collaborator`, `dependency`. Attempting to insert an invalid type is rejected by the Rust type system (enum validation before DB insert).
